WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.801
-You look fantastic, buddy.
Welcome back to the show.

00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:05.934
-I bought a fancy suit for you.
-You look great.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:07.567
Thank you so much
for dressing up.

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:09.868
-And congratulations, Questlove.
-Yeah, there you go.

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:12.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
The great.

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:14.100
-I mean,
it's so nice when somebody

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.701
wins the first time out.

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:20.234
-Anyway, Judd,
let's get to your...

00:00:20.234 --> 00:00:24.734
-I mean, some of us are
really try hard for 37 years.

00:00:24.734 --> 00:00:26.567
-But the first time out -- Yeah.
-Some people, they nail it.

00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:27.601
They nail it.

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:29.367
-There you go.
It's good for them.

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.767
-Too many boners in my movies.
I don't know.

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:35.834
I've got to eliminate
these boners.

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:37.767
-I want to talk to you about
"Sicker in the Head."

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:38.968
-Yes.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:40.501
-"Sick in the Head,"
the first book you did,

00:00:40.501 --> 00:00:42.067
a "New York Times" best seller.

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:43.467
I loved it so much.

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:47.000
I told you that I kept it on
my side table on my bed for like

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.934
a year and a half
because I wanted to --

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:50.133
I didn't want it to end.

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.634
All your interviews
are so interesting

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:53.234
with these great comedic minds.

00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:55.400
I'm like, "I don't want this
book to have an ending."

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.534
-And you were in it.

00:00:56.534 --> 00:00:58.367
-And I read
my part over and over again.

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.734
That's how sick I am
in my own head.

00:01:00.734 --> 00:01:02.767
When did you decide you
were going to do another book?

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:05.300
-Well, I was doing it
a little before the pandemic.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.067
-Okay.
-And I was being a little lazy.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.334
-Okay.
-And then the pandemic hit.

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:12.300
Then I realized
that everybody was home.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.200
-So they had no excuse.
-They had no excuse.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.000
-They have to do your interview.
-So I would call people.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.200
I'm like,
"I know you're available."

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:21.100
And all these people
I never would even ask,

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.234
you know, like Letterman.

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:23.267
Yeah, Letterman.

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:24.801
I'm like,
"You've got nothing to do."

00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:26.267
-Yeah, bud. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:28.267
"Let's do this right now."
"Come on, Whoopi."

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:29.634
-Whoopi Goldberg.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:31.067
But, also,
I just needed human contact,

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:32.601
so I just started calling,
you know,

00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:34.567
Lin-Manuel Miranda
and Nathan Fielder,

00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:35.767
and just talk to me, please.

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:37.133
-Yeah. Sacha Baron Cohen
is gonna --

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:38.234
-Yeah.

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:39.968
-You also have
non-comedians in there.

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:44.033
Roger Daltrey from The Who,
who is actually very funny.

00:01:44.033 --> 00:01:46.200
But he's --
But what an interesting guy.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.234
-Sure.

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:48.801
-You just are a Who fan
or what --

00:01:48.801 --> 00:01:52.267
-Yeah, and waited my entire life
to nerd out on him.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.901
-You did.
-Cameron Crowe is in there.

00:01:54.901 --> 00:01:56.300
Because I always felt, like,
a real connection to

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.834
Cameron Crowe, because I started
interviewing comedians

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:02.434
when I was 15 and he started
interviewing rock stars

00:02:02.434 --> 00:02:05.234
for, like, "Rolling Stone"
when he was 15, 16 years old.

00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:09.067
But it's different, because he's
so much cooler than I,

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.434
'cause he's --
because I'm, like, going --

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:13.701
I'm going to go drive
three hours to Poughkeepsie

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:15.801
to see "Weird Al" Yankovic,

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:18.300
and he's on tour with
Led Zeppelin.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.634
-But I see
the similarities there.

00:02:19.634 --> 00:02:21.567
-It's, like, similar, in a way.
-Very similar, yeah.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:23.100
It's Nana Week here at
"The Tonight Show."

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.267
We're talking about grandmas.

00:02:24.267 --> 00:02:28.901
And there's a great photo
in here of your grandma.

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.434
-Yes, Molly.
-Look at Molly.

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:31.567
-At my Molly.

00:02:31.567 --> 00:02:33.901
So, that's Totie Fields.
-The great Totie Fields.

00:02:33.901 --> 00:02:35.934
-The comedienne.
-I love Totie Fields.

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:38.601
-Yeah. And then my grandmother
was best buddies with her.

00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:40.400
And that's me next to
Totie Fields

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.567
at the Westbury Music Fair.

00:02:42.567 --> 00:02:45.334
-Let me see it. Wow!
Nice -- That's a great sweater.

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:46.767
-Yeah.
-Judd -- Yeah.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:48.067
At the Westbury Music Fair
with -- Wow!

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.334
-Yeah. And that's how
I got interested in comedy.

00:02:50.334 --> 00:02:52.267
She was friends
with Totie Fields.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:54.634
I remember I went to see her.
-Totie Fields, the legend.

00:02:54.634 --> 00:02:55.868
-Yeah, and she was like a --

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:58.434
you know, like, a
Joan Rivers type of comedienne.

00:02:58.434 --> 00:03:00.100
-Phyllis Diller. Yeah.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.567
-And she had diabetes
and she had her leg amputated.

00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:06.400
And then she did a comeback tour
at Westbury Music Fair

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.400
and she came out and did like
an hour of jokes about

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.100
having one leg, and the place
gave her a standing ovation.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.667
And I think, as a little kid,
I just thought like,

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:15.667
"Oh, my God.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:17.868
You can be cool in this world

00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:19.601
no matter what
the circumstances are."

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:21.300
-Yeah, you can do it.
That's what a comedian does.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.300
-Yeah.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:23.601
-I heard a rumor --

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:25.133
and I don't know if it's true,
but I hope it is --

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:27.400
that you're doing a sequel to
"This Is 40"

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.601
called "This Is 50."

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:30.434
-Yes. Do you want it?

00:03:30.434 --> 00:03:31.701
-Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:31.701 --> 00:03:34.901
-That's -- I'm trying to get
a feel for the room.

00:03:34.901 --> 00:03:38.133
-Yeah, I want it.
I would love to do it.

00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:39.834
Is it possible? Is it real?

00:03:39.834 --> 00:03:42.167
-I mean, I'm working on --
I have one big problem

00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:46.033
is that Paul Rudd
and Leslie don't age.

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:49.834
So, it's 10 years later.
They look exactly the same.

00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:51.167
And I think it would
annoy people, right?

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:52.367
-They would get mad.

00:03:52.367 --> 00:03:53.567
They'd go, "That's not what
50 looks like."

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:57.501
-Yeah, I could call it, like,
"This Is 42."

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.100
And you know what the worst part
about being friends with

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.534
Paul Rudd,
as the guy who doesn't age?

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:03.968
-Yeah.
-Is I age.

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:06.901
So every picture with him --
Like, I have pictures with him

00:04:06.901 --> 00:04:08.901
for the last like 20 years,

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:12.367
and he just doesn't change
and I'm slowly falling apart.

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:13.534
-Yeah.

00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:15.567
No, you look great.
You look -- Here's you --

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.601
-Here's us 10 years ago.
This is 10 years ago.

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:18.801
-This is you and Paul Rudd
10 years ago.

00:04:18.801 --> 00:04:21.567
-Okay. He looks
exactly the same right now.

00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:24.501
-He looks exactly the same.
-And I look like his dad.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.067
-Now I have to show
a picture that you --

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.267
This is you during the --

00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:32.234
-Well, this is --
I think -- Don't show it yet.

00:04:32.234 --> 00:04:35.767
I mean, during the pandemic,
we all lost it a little bit.

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:38.167
And, well, I kept growing beards

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:40.067
and different shapes of beards,

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:41.367
'cause there was
nothing else to do.

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:44.000
-Look at -- This is Judd Apatow,
good-looking Judd Apatow.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.501
This is you during the...

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.000
This is a low point.
This is "wow!"

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.434
This is probably
when you decided to write

00:04:51.434 --> 00:04:52.434
"Sicker in the Head."

00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:53.868
Here's Judd Apatow right here.

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:56.968
This is -- Holy mackerel!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:56.968 --> 00:05:00.067
-Yeah. There you go.

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:01.501
-That makes sense.

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.667
-Yeah.
-That makes sense.

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:06.601
Not only do you have
a big, giant book out,

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:08.667
but you also have a funny movie
called "The Bubble."

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:09.868
-Yes.

00:05:09.868 --> 00:05:12.300
-And I heard about this
as you were filming

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.167
because it was one of
the first things to

00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:15.367
kind of go in production.

00:05:15.367 --> 00:05:17.734
But I'm like,
"That's a great comedy mind."

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:19.467
You're like,
"People need entertainment.

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:20.634
That's what I do.

00:05:20.634 --> 00:05:22.767
I'm going to show up
and give it to them."

00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:25.334
Can you set up what
"The Bubble" is about?

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:27.801
-Well, you know,
I was taking these long walks

00:05:27.801 --> 00:05:31.901
every day to try to not go
insane and be more like that.

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:35.234
And, so, I kept thinking about
the NBA bubble.

00:05:35.234 --> 00:05:36.400
And I thought,
"Well, that would be, like,

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.767
a funny movie or a play."

00:05:37.767 --> 00:05:39.567
Like, a lot of 7-foot guys

00:05:39.567 --> 00:05:40.767
in a small space,
getting angry at each other.

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.000
-Yeah.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.434
-And then I thought,
"Oh, maybe, like, actors,"

00:05:43.434 --> 00:05:44.601
because you'd heard about, like,

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:47.167
the "Mission: Impossible"
problems and "Jurassic."

00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:49.200
And they were shooting,
like, "White Lotus,"

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.100
and all these people were
trying to stay in production.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.400
So I thought,
"Maybe you could do almost like

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.868
a Christopher Guest-type movie
of a bunch of actors

00:05:55.868 --> 00:05:57.534
stuck in a hotel, trying to make

00:05:57.534 --> 00:06:00.601
a flying-dinosaur action movie
during the pandemic.

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:02.734
And everything goes wrong,
and the studio will not

00:06:02.734 --> 00:06:05.667
let them leave until
they complete the movie."

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:07.033
-But does that --
I mean, flying --

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:08.901
They're great CGI, by the way.

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:10.234
It is amazing.

00:06:10.234 --> 00:06:12.133
But these are
very interesting dinosaurs.

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:14.634
I mean, this had to
cost millions to make these.

00:06:14.634 --> 00:06:15.734
-Yeah, at the beginning,
I thought,

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:17.000
"Maybe they should look cheap.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.367
And then I thought, "What if
they looked exactly like

00:06:19.367 --> 00:06:23.167
'Jurassic Park,' except they're
flying tyrannosaurus rexes?"

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:24.267
And I'm working with
the same people

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:26.334
who make the dinosaurs for
"Jurassic Park."

00:06:26.334 --> 00:06:28.934
And they love
that we're doing jokes,

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.534
because they never
get to do jokes.

00:06:30.534 --> 00:06:31.667
-You have to be -- Yeah.

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:32.834
-And by the end of it,
they're just like,

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:35.834
"What if the dinosaur's

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:39.701
penis and testicles
were flammable?

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:42.634
And then the penis
is like a fuse."

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.434
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-"And they blow up."

00:06:44.434 --> 00:06:45.934
-Okay, that's --
Now we got a comedy.

00:06:45.934 --> 00:06:47.000
Now we got a movie.
-And you know what I said.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.467
You know what I said.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.367
[Bleep] the Oscar."

00:06:51.367 --> 00:06:52.901
-Wow! Wow!

00:06:52.901 --> 00:06:55.901
He said, "I'm going for comedy.
I'm going for entertainment.

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:58.067
I'm going for entertainment."
That's exactly right.

00:06:58.067 --> 00:06:59.667
-If I said "no,"
I would have got it.

00:06:59.667 --> 00:07:01.367
-Exactly!
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:07:01.367 --> 00:07:02.901
Here is a scene from
"The Bubble."

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:04.701
Take a look at this.

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:06.501
-Gavin, how are you?

00:07:06.501 --> 00:07:09.767
-Hi. Hi. Hi. How you doing?

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:10.767
-Oh, good.

00:07:10.767 --> 00:07:13.133
I just wanted to say good luck

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:15.601
and we love you
and we're counting on you.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:17.133
-I know. I know. I'm on it.

00:07:17.133 --> 00:07:20.767
-You are such a hero for keeping
the lights on at the studio.

00:07:20.767 --> 00:07:25.067
This lockdown has been so hard
on all of us.

00:07:25.067 --> 00:07:26.734
-But -- but you're skiing.

00:07:26.734 --> 00:07:29.667
-Yeah, well, this was the only
country that was open.

00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:30.734
Thank God I got my shot.

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:31.934
-Did you?

00:07:31.934 --> 00:07:33.934
I thought they weren't
available for six months.

00:07:33.934 --> 00:07:35.968
-Oh, they're not --
Oh, not for normal people,

00:07:35.968 --> 00:07:37.300
but I'm with rich people.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.300
Don't hate the player.
Hate the game.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.100
[ Chuckles ]
Ugh! I hate the game.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.801
-"I hate the game."

00:07:42.801 --> 00:07:44.734
The one-and-only Judd Apatow,
everybody.

00:07:44.734 --> 00:07:47.601
"The Bubble"
is on Netflix Friday.

00:07:47.601 --> 00:07:49.133
And his book,
"Sicker in the Head,"

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:50.133
is a must-own.

00:07:50.133 --> 00:07:51.133
It's available now.

